### What is the problem?

Given a string, find the longest contiguous substring that reads the same forward and backward. This is the DP approach to the same problem solved by expand-around-center in Q1447. The DP version explicitly builds a table of which ranges are palindromes.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class LongestPalSubstringDP {
    public static String longestPalindrome(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        if (n < 2) return s;
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[n][n];
        int start = 0, maxLen = 1;

        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            dp[i][i] = true;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                if (s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j) && (j - i < 2 || dp[i + 1][j - 1])) {
                    dp[i][j] = true;
                    if (j - i + 1 > maxLen) {
                        maxLen = j - i + 1;
                        start = i;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return s.substring(start, start + maxLen);
    }
}
```

### Why use DP instead of expand-around-center?

The DP approach builds a reusable palindrome table that other problems can query in O(1). For example, palindromic partitioning needs to know whether arbitrary ranges are palindromes. The expand-around-center approach is better for standalone longest-palindrome queries because it uses O(1) space.

| Approach | Time | Space | Reusable table |
|---|---|---|---|
| DP table | O(n squared) | O(n squared) | Yes |
| Expand-around-center | O(n squared) | O(1) | No |
| Manacher's | O(n) | O(n) | Yes (radii) |

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class ComparisonDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "ABCBA";
        System.out.println("DP:     " + LongestPalSubstringDP.longestPalindrome(s));
        System.out.println("Expand: " + LongestPalindromicSubstring.longestPalindrome(s));
    }
}
```

```text
DP:     ABCBA
Expand: ABCBA
```

### How does the recurrence work?

A range [i, j] is a palindrome when:
1. The characters at both ends match: `s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j)`
2. Either the range is short (length 1 or 2) or the inner range [i+1, j-1] is also a palindrome.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class RecurrenceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "ABCBA";
        int n = s.length();
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[n][n];
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            dp[i][i] = true;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                boolean endsMatch = s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j);
                boolean innerOk = (j - i < 2) || dp[i + 1][j - 1];
                dp[i][j] = endsMatch && innerOk;
                if (dp[i][j]) {
                    System.out.println("Palindrome [" + i + "," + j + "]: " + s.substring(i, j + 1));
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Palindrome [0,4]: ABCBA
Palindrome [1,3]: BCB
```

### What is the fill order?

The table must be filled so that the inner range [i+1, j-1] is decided before the outer range [i, j]. This is achieved by iterating i from n-1 down to 0 and j from i+1 up to n-1. Alternatively, iterate by increasing range length.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class FillOrderDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "ABA";
        int n = s.length();
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[n][n];
        // Fill by decreasing i
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            dp[i][i] = true;
            System.out.println("Base: dp[" + i + "][" + i + "]=true");
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                dp[i][j] = s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j) && (j - i < 2 || dp[i + 1][j - 1]);
                System.out.println("dp[" + i + "][" + j + "]=" + dp[i][j]);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Base: dp[2][2]=true
dp[2][2]=true
Base: dp[1][1]=true
dp[1][2]=false
Base: dp[0][0]=true
dp[0][1]=false
dp[0][2]=true
```

### What are the edge cases?

An empty string returns empty. A single character returns itself. A string of all identical characters has the entire string as its longest palindrome. A string with no palindrome longer than 1 returns any single character.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class EdgeCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(LongestPalSubstringDP.longestPalindrome(""));     // ""
        System.out.println(LongestPalSubstringDP.longestPalindrome("A"));    // "A"
        System.out.println(LongestPalSubstringDP.longestPalindrome("AAAA")); // "AAAA"
        System.out.println(LongestPalSubstringDP.longestPalindrome("ABCD")); // "A"
    }
}
```

```text

A
AAAA
A
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

Time is O(n squared) because the table has n squared entries and each takes O(1) to compute. Space is O(n squared) for the boolean table. Expand-around-center achieves the same time with O(1) space, making it preferable when the table is not needed by other problems.

### How does this differ from the palindromic subsequence DP?

The palindromic substring DP uses a boolean table (is this range a palindrome?) while the palindromic subsequence DP uses an integer table (longest palindromic subsequence of this range). On a mismatch, the substring version sets false (contiguity broken), while the subsequence version takes the max of dropping one end (gaps allowed).

